      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "transparent": false,
      "description": "",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### How to use each page\n| Page | What it is for |\n|---|---|\n| 🏠 **Overview** | A summary of everything matching your filters, with shortcuts to each page. |\n| 🧭 **My Plan** | Enter savings, aid, borrowing and salary guesses; see cost, debt and payoff estimates. |\n| 🏫 **Schools** | Search schools, rank programs, and open a school's address and program details. |\n| 🗺️ **States & Taxes** | Verified income-tax facts for 10 states, with tax year and official source. |\n| 📊 **Graphs** | Five charts; each opens on its own page with an explanation. |\n| ❓ **Help** | This page. |\n\n### Search and filters\n- The boxes at the top (School, State, Major, CIP code, Credential, School type, Data year) are **shared**: your choices follow you to every page through the navigation bar.\n- **School** is searchable: click it and type part of a name, e.g. *\"state univ\"*. The list narrows to schools matching your other filters.\n- Choose **All** to remove a filter. If a page says *No data*, that filter combination has no published records — widen it.\n- In the Schools table, click a school's name to open its details.",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      },
      "id": 3,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 5,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 15
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How the metrics are calculated",
      "options": {
        "content": "### Metric Formulas\n\n| Metric | Formula | Interpretation |\n|--------|---------|----------------|\n| **Debt / Earnings** | `median_debt ÷ median_earnings` | Lower = less debt relative to one year of income |\n| **Earnings / Debt** | `median_earnings ÷ median_debt` | Higher = more annual income relative to total debt |\n| **Earnings − Debt** | `median_earnings − median_debt` | Positive = annual earnings exceed total cumulative debt |\n\n**Color thresholds (Debt/Earnings):** 🟢 < 0.5 · 🟡 0.5–1.0 · 🔴 ≥ 1.0\n\nAll values are medians — half of completers were above, half below.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 12,
        "y": 5,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 8
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "transparent": false,
      "description": "",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### The three career scenarios (My Plan)\n- **Conservative** — a starting salary you are fairly sure you could beat.\n- **Expected** — your best guess. If blank, GradPath uses the historical median for your school and major, labelled as such.\n- **Aspirational** — a strong outcome, not a promise.\n\nMonthly payment is the larger of the 10-year standard payment and the share of salary you choose (10% by default). Payoff dates are worked out month by month with a fixed interest rate.",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      },
      "id": 5,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 20,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 7
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "transparent": false,
      "description": "",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Median earnings\nThe middle salary of graduates who were working and not enrolled, measured **2 years after completion** (`EARN_MDN_HI_2YR`). Half earned more, half less.\n\n### Median debt\nThe middle amount of **federal** student-loan debt at completion (`DEBT_ALL_PP_ANY_MDN`). It is **not** the cost of attendance and leaves out private loans and Parent PLUS loans.\n\n### Debt-to-earnings ratio\n`median debt ÷ median earnings`. **0.5** means debt equal to half a year of earnings. Colour bands: 🟢 under 0.5 · 🟡 0.5–1.0 · 🔴 1.0 or more. They help scanning; they are not a grade.\n\n### Data years\n- Program outcomes: **2021–22 completers (earnings measured 2023–24)** (College Scorecard release 2026-10-05, downloaded 2026-11-02).\n- School addresses: College Scorecard Institution file, same release.\n- State taxes: **tax year 2025**, verified 2026-11-04.\n- Default loan rate: **6.52%**, federal undergraduate Direct Loans first disbursed July 1, 2026–June 30, 2027.",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      },
      "id": 6,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 12,
        "y": 20,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 15
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "transparent": false,
      "description": "",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Tax-data methodology\n- Each state's facts were checked on that state's **official revenue website** (or official booklet). The URL and date are stored with every row in `state_tax_reference.csv`.\n- A field is filled only when the official page states it; otherwise it shows **Not verified**. GradPath never copies one state's rules to another.\n- California and Massachusetts official pages block automated downloads, so their figures were read through a search index of those official pages. Open their source links to confirm.\n- Estimates assume a single filer with wage income only, use the verified standard deduction, and ignore credits and local taxes.\n\n### Privacy\n- GradPath does not ask for bank accounts, logins or account numbers.\n- **My Plan** answers are kept in your browser (localStorage) and in the page address. The API computes estimates on request and does not save them. Use *Clear my answers* on the form to remove them.\n- Sharing a My Plan link shares the numbers in it.",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      },
      "id": 7,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 35,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 12
      }
    },
